11
00:00:37.583 --> 00:00:39.503
Introduction and Why It Matters.

12
00:00:40.753 --> 00:00:43.612
A story: the double charge nobody could explain.

13
00:00:43.892 --> 00:00:53.172
During a Sunday-morning peak, the grocery app's checkout call started taking 6 to 9 seconds, because a slow promotion service sat on the critical path.

14
00:00:53.452 --> 00:00:57.569
The mobile client had a 5-second timeout and retried automatically.

15
00:00:57.849 --> 00:00:59.279
The server hadn't failed.

16
00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:00.711
It was just slow.

17
00:01:00.991 --> 00:01:08.521
So each failed checkout had actually succeeded, and the retry created a second order and a second UPI collect request.

18
00:01:08.801 --> 00:01:10.572
Some customers approved both.

19
00:01:10.852 --> 00:01:16.996
By noon, support had 2,300 tickets about duplicate orders and duplicate payments.

20
00:01:17.276 --> 00:01:20.881
Finance spent two weeks reconciling refunds with Razorpay.

21
00:01:22.231 --> 00:01:24.962
The root cause wasn't the slow promotion service.

22
00:01:25.242 --> 00:01:26.991
It was the API contract:

23
00:01:27.271 --> 00:01:33.991
POST or orders had no idempotency key, so the server couldn't tell a new order from the same order, asked twice.

24
00:01:34.271 --> 00:01:38.858
The network always delivers some requests twice, because timeouts are ambiguous.

25
00:01:39.138 --> 00:01:42.509
An API that can't handle that is broken by design.

26
00:01:43.859 --> 00:01:44.541
Quote:

27
00:01:44.821 --> 00:01:46.485
The thesis of this chapter.

28
00:01:46.765 --> 00:01:55.043
An API is a long-lived contract with clients you don't control, over a network that duplicates, reorders and drops messages.

29
00:01:55.323 --> 00:02:01.061
Good API design makes the contract safe to retry, safe to evolve, and hard to misuse.

30
00:02:01.341 --> 00:02:04.925
The contract usually outlives the implementation behind it.

31
