330
00:15:50.475 --> 00:15:53.739
One client's key can't collide with, or reveal, another's.

331
00:15:54.019 --> 00:15:56.877
Store a fingerprint of the request (hash of the body):

332
00:15:57.157 --> 00:15:59.824
Same key with a different body is a client bug.

333
00:16:00.104 --> 00:16:03.581
Return 422, don't replay the wrong result.

334
00:16:03.861 --> 00:16:07.168
Insert the key before doing the work, with a unique constraint:

335
00:16:07.448 --> 00:16:09.347
Two concurrent duplicates race.

336
00:16:09.627 --> 00:16:10.992
Only one may proceed.

337
00:16:11.272 --> 00:16:15.240
While in progress, duplicates get 409 + Retry-After:

338
00:16:15.520 --> 00:16:18.165
Don't run twice, and don't block a thread waiting.

339
00:16:18.445 --> 00:16:21.688
On completion, store the response (status + body):

340
00:16:21.968 --> 00:16:24.293
Retries get byte-identical replays.

341
00:16:25.643 --> 00:16:29.910
On retriable failure (5xx before side effects), release the key:

342
00:16:30.190 --> 00:16:32.409
So the client can retry the operation.

343
00:16:32.689 --> 00:16:35.483
Expire keys after a window (24 h):

344
00:16:35.763 --> 00:16:36.958
Bounded storage.

345
00:16:37.238 --> 00:16:38.518
Document the window.

346
00:16:38.798 --> 00:16:40.910
Keys don't replace business uniqueness:

347
00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:45.350
Still have unique constraints on things like one payment per order attempt.

348
00:16:46.700 --> 00:16:57.452
The IETF draft for the Idempotency-Key HTTP header standardizes the header name and the 409 or 422 semantics described here.

