359
00:17:22.917 --> 00:17:23.771
Benefit:

360
00:17:24.051 --> 00:17:27.635
Saves bandwidth and CPU (catalog, category trees).

361
00:17:27.915 --> 00:17:29.877
Optimistic concurrency (write).

362
00:17:30.157 --> 00:17:31.203
Request header:

363
00:17:31.483 --> 00:17:33.040
If-Match: v17.

364
00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:34.536
Server behaviour:

365
00:17:34.816 --> 00:17:37.781
Different, then 412 Precondition Failed.

366
00:17:38.061 --> 00:17:38.915
Benefit:

367
00:17:39.195 --> 00:17:40.795
Prevents the lost update.

368
00:17:41.075 --> 00:17:42.547
Require a precondition.

369
00:17:42.827 --> 00:17:44.555
Request header: none sent.

370
00:17:44.835 --> 00:17:46.051
Server behaviour:

371
00:17:46.331 --> 00:17:49.019
428 Precondition Required.

372
00:17:49.299 --> 00:17:50.152
Benefit:

373
00:17:50.432 --> 00:17:52.672
Forces clients to be concurrency-safe.

374
00:17:54.022 --> 00:17:56.710
The lost update without ETags looks like this.

375
00:17:56.990 --> 00:18:01.726
The customer opens an order (status PLACED) and taps Cancel 40 seconds later.

376
00:18:02.006 --> 00:18:03.542
Meanwhile the store packed it.

377
00:18:03.822 --> 00:18:09.838
Without If-Match, the server may cancel a packed order, or a naive PUT may overwrite the packing status.

378
00:18:10.118 --> 00:18:16.667
With If-Match, the server answers 412, and the app re-fetches and shows Your order is already packed.

379
00:18:16.947 --> 00:18:21.491
Strong versus weak ETags: v17 is strong (byte-identical).

380
00:18:21.771 --> 00:18:25.526
W/v17 is weak (semantically equivalent).

381
00:18:25.806 --> 00:18:33.998
For concurrency control use strong ETags derived from the entity version (JPA @Version), not from a hash of the JSON.

