00:26:54.627 --> 00:26:55.971
Here is the diagram.

523
00:26:59.821 --> 00:27:01.165
Here is a quick table:

524
00:27:01.445 --> 00:27:04.730
API gateway, and Backend-for-Frontend (BFF).

525
00:27:05.010 --> 00:27:05.842
Purpose.

526
00:27:06.122 --> 00:27:07.402
API gateway:

527
00:27:07.682 --> 00:27:09.303
Cross-cutting edge concerns.

528
00:27:09.583 --> 00:27:11.610
Backend-for-Frontend (BFF):

529
00:27:11.890 --> 00:27:13.597
Client-specific composition.

530
00:27:13.877 --> 00:27:14.687
Does.

531
00:27:14.967 --> 00:27:16.247
API gateway:

532
00:27:16.527 --> 00:27:23.951
TLS, authentication, rate limiting, routing, request size limits, WAF integration, observability.

533
00:27:24.231 --> 00:27:26.258
Backend-for-Frontend (BFF):

534
00:27:26.538 --> 00:27:32.533
Aggregates calls per screen, shapes payloads, handles app-version quirks, orchestrates UI flows.

535
00:27:32.813 --> 00:27:33.709
Owned by.

536
00:27:33.989 --> 00:27:35.269
API gateway:

537
00:27:35.549 --> 00:27:36.594
Platform team.

538
00:27:36.874 --> 00:27:38.901
Backend-for-Frontend (BFF):

539
00:27:39.181 --> 00:27:42.018
The client team (web team owns web-bff).

540
00:27:42.298 --> 00:27:43.450
Business logic.

541
00:27:43.730 --> 00:27:45.010
API gateway:

542
00:27:45.290 --> 00:27:45.973
None.

543
00:27:46.253 --> 00:27:48.279
Backend-for-Frontend (BFF):

544
00:27:48.559 --> 00:27:51.802
Presentation logic only, never domain rules.

545
00:27:52.082 --> 00:27:52.807
Count.

546
00:27:53.087 --> 00:27:54.367
API gateway:

547
00:27:54.647 --> 00:27:57.997
One (or one per trust zone: public, partner, internal).

548
00:27:58.277 --> 00:28:00.303
Backend-for-Frontend (BFF):

549
00:28:00.583 --> 00:28:01.906
One per client type.

550
00:28:03.256 --> 00:28:04.301
Anti-pattern.

551
00:28:04.581 --> 00:28:05.861
API gateway:

552
00:28:06.141 --> 00:28:08.872
Gateway with business logic (smart gateway).

553
00:28:09.152 --> 00:28:11.179
Backend-for-Frontend (BFF):

554
00:28:11.459 --> 00:28:14.936
BFF that becomes a second monolith with domain rules.

555
00:28:16.286 --> 00:28:18.910
Webhooks: design for senders and receivers.

556
00:28:19.190 --> 00:28:24.225
Webhooks are reverse APIs: you call the consumer's endpoint when something happens.

557
00:28:24.505 --> 00:28:28.985
As a sender (grocery, then seller systems: order.placed, order.cancelled):

558
00:28:30.335 --> 00:28:31.679
Here is a quick table:

559
00:28:31.959 --> 00:28:33.345
Concern, and Design.

560
00:28:33.625 --> 00:28:34.628
Authenticity:

561
00:28:34.908 --> 00:28:46.279
HMAC-SHA256 signature over timestamp +. + rawBody with a per-endpoint secret; header Grocery-Signature: t=...v1=...

562
00:28:46.559 --> 00:28:47.775
Replay protection:

563
00:28:48.055 --> 00:28:51.148
Receivers reject timestamps older than 5 minutes.

564
00:28:51.428 --> 00:28:52.303
Delivery:

565
00:28:52.583 --> 00:29:01.201
At-least-once from an outbox (Ch 13), retried with exponential backoff + jitter for ~24 to 72 h.

566
00:29:01.481 --> 00:29:02.591
Idempotency:

567
00:29:02.871 --> 00:29:07.223
Each delivery has a stable event.id, and receivers dedupe on it.

568
00:29:07.503 --> 00:29:08.292
Ordering:

569
00:29:08.572 --> 00:29:09.681
Not guaranteed.

570
00:29:09.961 --> 00:29:15.764
Include sequence or occurredAt and the resource version, and receivers fetch current state if unsure.

571
00:29:16.044 --> 00:29:16.897
Payload:

572
00:29:17.177 --> 00:29:21.977
Thin (order ord42 changed, version 7) or fat (full snapshot).

573
00:29:22.257 --> 00:29:25.415
Thin plus fetch is safer for PII and evolution.

574
00:29:26.765 --> 00:29:27.746
Endpoint health:

575
00:29:28.026 --> 00:29:34.938
Disable after N consecutive days of failures and notify the seller; provide a replay UI or API.

576
00:29:35.218 --> 00:29:36.050
Timeouts:

577
00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:37.887
Short (5 to 10 s).

578
00:29:38.167 --> 00:29:41.005
The receiver should acknowledge fast and process async.

579
00:29:42.355 --> 00:29:45.085
As a receiver (Razorpay, then payment-service):

580
00:29:45.365 --> 00:29:52.363
Verify the signature over the raw body bytes (not re-serialized JSON) with a constant-time comparison.

581
00:29:52.643 --> 00:29:56.632
Deduplicate by event or payment id (idempotent processing).

582
00:29:56.912 --> 00:30:00.411
Respond 2xx quickly, then process asynchronously.

583
00:30:00.691 --> 00:30:02.845
Never trust the payload alone for money.

584
00:30:03.125 --> 00:30:07.243
Fetch or verify the payment status via the API when in doubt.

585
00:30:08.593 --> 00:30:13.990
Tolerate out-of-order events (a payment.captured may arrive before payment.authorized).

586
00:30:15.340 --> 00:30:17.089
Designing for mobile clients.

587
00:30:17.369 --> 00:30:18.713
Here is a quick table:

588
00:30:18.993 --> 00:30:20.337
Concern, and Practice.

589
00:30:20.617 --> 00:30:23.561
Round trips are expensive (Ch 03):

590
00:30:23.841 --> 00:30:28.065
Screen-shaped BFF endpoints; one call per screen where possible.

591
00:30:28.345 --> 00:30:29.455
Payload size:

592
00:30:29.735 --> 00:30:34.663
Compact DTOs, fields= or GraphQL, compression, no nulls-everywhere.

593
00:30:34.943 --> 00:30:36.159
Old app versions:

594
00:30:36.439 --> 00:30:41.409
Tolerant parsing, server-driven minimum version, feature flags by app version.

595
00:30:41.689 --> 00:30:42.841
Flaky networks:

596
00:30:43.121 --> 00:30:47.537
Idempotency keys on every mutating call; safe automatic retries.

597
00:30:47.817 --> 00:30:49.268
Clock skew on devices:

598
00:30:49.548 --> 00:30:54.391
Server timestamps are authoritative; never trust client time for business rules.

599
00:30:54.671 --> 00:30:56.121
Unknown enum values:

600
00:30:56.401 --> 00:31:00.583
Clients map unknowns to a default (OTHER), enforced by code review.

601
00:31:01.933 --> 00:31:02.829
