722
00:38:24.627 --> 00:38:26.141
Alternatives considered.

723
00:38:27.491 --> 00:38:28.835
Here is a quick table:

724
00:38:29.115 --> 00:38:31.654
Option, and Pros, and Cons, and Verdict.

725
00:38:31.934 --> 00:38:33.769
Rely on client not retrying.

726
00:38:34.049 --> 00:38:34.902
Pros:

727
00:38:35.182 --> 00:38:36.313
Nothing to build.

728
00:38:36.593 --> 00:38:37.403
Cons:

729
00:38:37.683 --> 00:38:41.033
Networks retry for you (proxies, SDKs, users).

730
00:38:41.313 --> 00:38:42.123
Verdict:

731
00:38:42.403 --> 00:38:43.321
Rejected.

732
00:38:43.601 --> 00:38:46.054
Business dedupe only (one order per cart).

733
00:38:46.334 --> 00:38:47.187
Pros:

734
00:38:47.467 --> 00:38:48.235
Simple.

735
00:38:48.515 --> 00:38:49.326
Cons:

736
00:38:49.606 --> 00:38:54.299
A cart can legitimately be re-ordered; doesn't cover cancel or refund actions.

737
00:38:54.579 --> 00:38:55.390
Verdict:

738
00:38:55.670 --> 00:38:56.715
Complementary.

739
00:38:56.995 --> 00:38:58.467
Idempotency in Redis.

740
00:38:58.747 --> 00:38:59.601
Pros:

741
00:38:59.881 --> 00:39:00.670
Fast.

742
00:39:00.950 --> 00:39:01.761
Cons:

743
00:39:02.041 --> 00:39:05.795
Harder to make atomic with the order write; eviction loses keys.

744
00:39:06.075 --> 00:39:06.886
Verdict:

745
00:39:07.166 --> 00:39:08.745
Rejected for money flows.

746
00:39:09.025 --> 00:39:14.145
Idempotency table in the same DB as orders, same transaction for completion.

747
00:39:14.425 --> 00:39:15.278
Pros:

748
00:39:15.558 --> 00:39:17.841
Atomic with the business write; durable.

749
00:39:18.121 --> 00:39:18.931
Cons:

750
00:39:19.211 --> 00:39:20.790
Extra write per request.

751
00:39:21.070 --> 00:39:21.881
Verdict:

752
00:39:22.161 --> 00:39:22.993
Chosen.

753
00:39:24.343 --> 00:39:25.687
Offset pagination.

754
00:39:25.967 --> 00:39:26.820
Pros:

755
00:39:27.100 --> 00:39:28.593
Simple, page numbers.

756
00:39:28.873 --> 00:39:29.684
Cons:

757
00:39:29.964 --> 00:39:32.119
O(offset), unstable pages.

758
00:39:32.399 --> 00:39:33.209
Verdict:

759
00:39:33.489 --> 00:39:34.855
Admin UIs only.

760
00:39:35.135 --> 00:39:37.055
Keyset with opaque signed cursor.

761
00:39:37.335 --> 00:39:38.188
Pros:

762
00:39:38.468 --> 00:39:39.876
O(limit), stable.

763
00:39:40.156 --> 00:39:40.967
Cons:

764
00:39:41.247 --> 00:39:42.697
No jump to page N.

765
00:39:42.977 --> 00:39:43.788
Verdict:

766
00:39:44.068 --> 00:39:44.900
Chosen.

767
00:39:46.250 --> 00:39:46.933
8.

768
00:39:47.213 --> 00:39:48.365
Failure modes.

769
00:39:48.645 --> 00:39:53.999
A pod crashes mid-request: the record stays INPROGRESS until lockeduntil (30 s).

770
00:39:54.279 --> 00:40:01.063
The next retry takes over and checks whether an order already exists for the key's resourceid before re-executing.

771
00:40:01.343 --> 00:40:02.687
DB unavailable:

772
00:40:02.967 --> 00:40:08.066
503 with a retryable problem, and the client retries with the same key.

773
00:40:08.346 --> 00:40:13.423
Clock skew between pods: lockeduntil uses DB time (now()), not pod time.

774
00:40:13.703 --> 00:40:14.450
9.

775
00:40:14.730 --> 00:40:15.669
Security.

776
00:40:15.949 --> 00:40:18.594
Keys are scoped to the authenticated customer.

777
00:40:18.874 --> 00:40:22.351
Cursors are HMAC-signed (tampering returns 400).

778
00:40:22.631 --> 00:40:25.149
Order ids are opaque (no enumeration).

