10.

1280
01:09:06.967 --> 01:09:09.121
Clients switching on error message text.

1281
01:09:09.401 --> 01:09:10.212
Symptom:

1282
01:09:10.492 --> 01:09:12.796
Fixing a typo in detail breaks the app.

1283
01:09:13.076 --> 01:09:13.801
Fix:

1284
01:09:14.081 --> 01:09:17.153
Stable type and code. detail is for humans.

1285
01:09:18.503 --> 01:09:19.378
Part 8.

1286
01:09:19.658 --> 01:09:21.002
Interview Q and A.

1287
01:09:22.252 --> 01:09:23.084
Beginner.

1288
01:09:23.364 --> 01:09:24.388
Question 1.

1289
01:09:24.668 --> 01:09:27.505
How do you model REST resources for an orders system?

1290
01:09:27.785 --> 01:09:29.151
Thirty-second answer:

1291
01:09:29.431 --> 01:09:36.620
Use nouns and collections: /orders, /orders/id, /orders/id/items.

1292
01:09:36.900 --> 01:09:58.788
Use HTTP methods for their semantics (GET read, POST create, PUT replace, PATCH partial update, DELETE remove), explicit action endpoints for business transitions (POST or orders/id/cancel), opaque ids, money in minor units with currency, and ISO-8601 UTC timestamps.

1293
01:10:00.138 --> 01:10:01.141
Question 2.

1294
01:10:01.421 --> 01:10:04.130
What's the difference between PUT, POST and PATCH?

1295
01:10:04.410 --> 01:10:05.733
Which are idempotent?

1296
01:10:06.013 --> 01:10:07.378
Thirty-second answer:

1297
01:10:07.658 --> 01:10:11.797
POST creates or triggers an action and is not idempotent by default.

1298
01:10:12.077 --> 01:10:17.879
PUT replaces a resource at a known URI and is idempotent (same request, same result).

1299
01:10:18.159 --> 01:10:23.493
PATCH partially updates and isn't necessarily idempotent (for example increment quantity).

1300
01:10:23.773 --> 01:10:27.143
GET, HEAD, PUT and DELETE are idempotent by definition.

1301
01:10:28.493 --> 01:10:29.539
Question 3.

1302
01:10:29.819 --> 01:10:39.632
Explain 401 versus 403 versus 404, and 409 versus 412 versus 422.

1303
01:10:39.912 --> 01:10:41.277
Thirty-second answer:

1304
01:10:41.557 --> 01:10:44.160
401 means not authenticated.

1305
01:10:44.440 --> 01:10:47.619
403 means authenticated but not allowed.

1306
01:10:47.899 --> 01:10:54.512
404 means not found, and it's also used to hide the existence of resources the caller mustn't see.

1307
01:10:54.792 --> 01:11:00.616
409 means a conflict with current state (can't cancel a packed order; key in progress).

1308
01:11:00.896 --> 01:11:05.163
412 means an If-Match precondition failed (stale version).

1309
01:11:05.443 --> 01:11:14.552
422 means well-formed but semantically invalid (pincode not serviceable; idempotency key reused with a different body).

1310
01:11:15.902 --> 01:11:16.969
Question 4.

1311
01:11:17.249 --> 01:11:20.235
Offset versus cursor pagination: which and why?

1312
01:11:20.515 --> 01:11:21.881
Thirty-second answer:

1313
01:11:22.161 --> 01:11:30.182
Offset is simple and supports jump to page N, but costs O(offset) and returns duplicates or skips when data changes.

1314
01:11:30.462 --> 01:11:34.494
Cursor (keyset) pagination seeks from the last item's sort key:

1315
01:11:34.774 --> 01:11:39.339
O(limit) at any depth, and stable under inserts, but only next or prev.

1316
01:11:39.619 --> 01:11:42.499
Use cursor for APIs, feeds and history.

1317
01:11:42.779 --> 01:11:44.870
Use offset for small admin tables.

1318
01:11:46.220 --> 01:11:47.223
Intermediate.

1319
01:11:47.503 --> 01:11:48.612
Question 5.

1320
01:11:48.892 --> 01:11:51.687
Design an idempotent 'create order' API.

1321
01:11:51.967 --> 01:11:53.332
Thirty-second answer:

1322
01:11:53.612 --> 01:12:00.375
The client sends an Idempotency-Key (a UUID per logical attempt, persisted locally for retries).

1323
01:12:00.655 --> 01:12:19.236
The server scopes it per customer, stores a fingerprint of the request, claims the key with a unique insert before doing work, completes the key in the same transaction as the order write with the stored response, replays that response on retries, returns 409 + Retry-After while in progress,

1324
01:12:19.516 --> 01:12:25.767
returns 422 on reuse with a different body, and expires keys after 24 h.

1325
01:12:27.117 --> 01:12:28.226
Question 6.

1326
01:12:28.506 --> 01:12:30.277
What problem do ETags solve?

1327
01:12:30.557 --> 01:12:31.922
Thirty-second answer:

1328
01:12:32.202 --> 01:12:33.205
Two problems.

1329
01:12:33.485 --> 01:12:34.317
Caching:

1330
01:12:34.597 --> 01:12:37.647
If-None-Match, then 304 saves bandwidth.

1331
01:12:37.927 --> 01:12:38.909
Concurrency:

1332
01:12:39.189 --> 01:12:48.661
If-Match on writes prevents lost updates, because if the resource changed since the client read it, the server returns 412 and the client re-fetches.

1333
01:12:50.011 --> 01:12:51.099
Question 7.

1334
01:12:51.379 --> 01:12:55.176
How do you version an API, and what counts as a breaking change?

1335
01:12:55.456 --> 01:12:56.821
Thirty-second answer:

1336
01:12:57.101 --> 01:13:04.675
Prefer additive evolution within a major version, with URI major versions (/v1) for public APIs.

1337
01:13:04.955 --> 01:13:20.315
Breaking changes: removing or renaming fields, changing types or semantics, making optional fields required, changing status codes, error types, pagination or defaults, and adding enum values that clients switch on exhaustively.

1338
01:13:20.595 --> 01:13:23.731
Deprecate with headers, metrics and a sunset timeline.

1339
01:13:25.081 --> 01:13:26.105
Question 8.

1340
01:13:26.385 --> 01:13:30.502
REST versus GraphQL versus gRPC: how do you choose?

1341
01:13:30.782 --> 01:13:32.147
Thirty-second answer:

1342
01:13:32.427 --> 01:13:38.827
REST or JSON for public and partner APIs: cacheable, universal, easy to document.

1343
01:13:39.107 --> 01:13:52.739
GraphQL for client-driven aggregation across many services and screens, with batching and cost limits. gRPC for high-performance internal service calls with strict contracts, streaming and deadlines.

1344
01:13:54.089 --> 01:13:55.156
Question 9.

1345
01:13:55.436 --> 01:13:58.892
Design a consistent error model for 26 services.

1346
01:13:59.172 --> 01:14:00.537
Thirty-second answer:

1347
01:14:00.817 --> 01:14:16.561
RFC 9457 Problem Details everywhere (application or problem+json), with a stable type URI and a machine code, a human detail, correlationId, a retryable flag, and field-level errors.

1348
01:14:16.841 --> 01:14:18.463
Document codes in the spec.

1349
01:14:18.743 --> 01:14:21.644
Clients branch on code, never on message text.

1350
01:14:22.994 --> 01:14:23.762
Senior.

1351
01:14:24.042 --> 01:14:25.087
Question 10.

1352
01:14:25.367 --> 01:14:29.549
Your idempotency implementation still produced duplicates under load.

1353
01:14:29.829 --> 01:14:30.810
What went wrong?

1354
01:14:31.090 --> 01:14:32.455
Thirty-second answer:

1355
01:14:32.735 --> 01:14:53.279
Likely causes: check-then-insert without a unique constraint (a race); the key committed only after the work, so duplicates didn't see it; key completion in a different transaction from the business write (crash between the two); keys stored in an evicting cache; keys not scoped (or scoped per pod); or clients generating a new key per retry.

1356
01:14:54.629 --> 01:14:55.760
Question 11.

1357
01:14:56.040 --> 01:15:00.200
How do you implement keyset pagination with sorting and filters at scale?

1358
01:15:00.480 --> 01:15:01.845
Thirty-second answer:

1359
01:15:02.125 --> 01:15:08.589
Allow-list sort orders, each with a composite index matching (filter columns... sort columns... id).

1360
01:15:08.869 --> 01:15:12.859
Encode the last row's sort values in a signed, versioned cursor.

1361
01:15:13.139 --> 01:15:17.939
Use row-value comparison ((a, b) (??)) with the same direction as the ORDER BY.

1362
01:15:18.219 --> 01:15:19.648
Fetch limit + 1.

1363
01:15:19.928 --> 01:15:24.280
For complex search, use a search engine's searchafter rather than SQL.

1364
01:15:25.630 --> 01:15:26.718
Question 12.

1365
01:15:26.998 --> 01:15:29.387
Design webhooks from the platform to sellers.

1366
01:15:29.667 --> 01:15:31.033
Thirty-second answer:

1367
01:15:31.313 --> 01:15:36.134
Produce events from an outbox, and create one delivery row per (event, endpoint).

1368
01:15:36.414 --> 01:15:58.771
Sign with HMAC-SHA256 over timestamp + raw body, deliver at least once with exponential backoff and jitter for ~72 h, use a stable event id for receiver dedupe, apply short timeouts and per-endpoint concurrency limits, dead-letter with notification, and provide a replay API and a delivery log UI.

1369
01:16:00.121 --> 01:16:01.401
Question 13.

1370
01:16:01.681 --> 01:16:06.972
GraphQL: what are the N+1 problem and query cost, and how do you control them?

1371
01:16:07.252 --> 01:16:08.617
Thirty-second answer:

1372
01:16:08.897 --> 01:16:16.727
N+1 happens when resolving a field per item calls the backend N times (20 orders, then 20 rider lookups).

1373
01:16:17.007 --> 01:16:21.913
Fix it with batching (DataLoader or @BatchMapping) and caching within a request.

1374
01:16:22.193 --> 01:16:32.711
Query cost: clients can request deep or wide graphs, so enforce depth and complexity limits, timeouts, persisted queries (an allow-list) and per-client rate limits.

1375
01:16:34.061 --> 01:16:35.255
Question 14.

1376
01:16:35.535 --> 01:16:39.077
How do you design rate limits and quotas for the seller API?

1377
01:16:39.357 --> 01:16:40.722
Thirty-second answer:

1378
01:16:41.002 --> 01:17:00.053
Per seller API key: a token bucket per endpoint class (reads versus writes), daily quotas for bulk operations, 429 with Retry-After and rate-limit headers (RateLimit-Limit, RateLimit-Remaining, RateLimit-Reset), separate limits for expensive endpoints, and plan-based tiers.

1379
01:17:00.333 --> 01:17:05.154
Enforce at the gateway with a distributed store (Redis), with local burst allowance.

1380
01:17:06.504 --> 01:17:07.400
Architect.

1381
01:17:07.680 --> 01:17:08.875
Question 15.

1382
01:17:09.155 --> 01:17:14.509
How would you run API governance across 26 services and several client teams?

1383
01:17:14.789 --> 01:17:16.155
Thirty-second answer:

1384
01:17:16.435 --> 01:17:21.107
A lightweight API guild owns a style guide, encoded as Spectral rules.

1385
01:17:21.387 --> 01:17:24.160
Specs are contract-first in each service repo.

1386
01:17:24.440 --> 01:17:27.256
CI enforces lint and breaking-change diffs.

1387
01:17:27.536 --> 01:17:33.339
Shared starters cover Problem Details, idempotency, pagination and correlation ids.

1388
01:17:33.619 --> 01:17:35.987
Clients and SDKs are generated.

1389
01:17:36.267 --> 01:17:43.157
There's a deprecation policy with metrics, and reviews only for new public or seller APIs or major versions.

1390
01:17:44.507 --> 01:17:45.702
Question 16.

1391
01:17:45.982 --> 01:17:52.062
API gateway, BFF, or GraphQL federation: how do you decide for the customer apps?

1392
01:17:52.342 --> 01:17:53.707
Thirty-second answer:

1393
01:17:53.987 --> 01:17:56.953
Always have a gateway for cross-cutting edge concerns.

1394
01:17:57.233 --> 01:18:04.379
Add BFFs when web and mobile need differently shaped, screen-oriented APIs and the client teams can own them.

1395
01:18:04.659 --> 01:18:16.243
Choose GraphQL (federated) when many client teams and screens need flexible composition across many services and you can invest in schema governance and query-cost controls.

1396
01:18:17.593 --> 01:18:18.937
Question 17.

1397
01:18:19.217 --> 01:18:26.428
You must retire or v1 or orders or history but 8 % of traffic comes from app versions over a year old.

1398
01:18:26.708 --> 01:18:27.689
What's your plan?

1399
01:18:27.969 --> 01:18:29.335
Thirty-second answer:

1400
01:18:29.615 --> 01:18:52.868
Measure by app version, keep v1 working behind an adapter onto the new implementation, add Deprecation or Sunset headers, ship an in-app force-upgrade prompt via the minimum-supported-version config, run brownouts (scheduled short windows returning 410 with a friendly message), coordinate with product on a sunset date, and only then return 410.

1401
01:18:54.218 --> 01:18:55.391
Question 18.

1402
01:18:55.671 --> 01:18:58.573
Design the developer platform for third-party sellers.

1403
01:18:58.853 --> 01:19:00.218
Thirty-second answer:

1404
01:19:00.498 --> 01:19:21.789
OAuth2 client credentials with scopes (catalog:write, orders:read), a sandbox with test data and simulated events, OpenAPI docs and generated SDKs, signed webhooks with a replay API, rate limits and quotas by tier, async bulk jobs (202 + job resource), a status page and changelog,

1405
01:19:22.069 --> 01:19:28.618
versioning and deprecation policy, and self-serve dashboards (API usage, webhook deliveries, errors).

1406
01:19:29.968 --> 01:19:31.205
Question 19.

1407
01:19:31.485 --> 01:19:35.987
Should internal service-to-service calls standardize on gRPC or REST?

1408
01:19:36.267 --> 01:19:37.632
Thirty-second answer:

1409
01:19:37.912 --> 01:19:49.603
It depends on team skills, tooling and performance needs. gRPC gives typed contracts, efficient encoding, streaming and deadlines, which suit high-QPS internal paths.

1410
01:19:49.883 --> 01:19:53.872
REST or JSON gives universal tooling and easier debugging.

1411
01:19:54.152 --> 01:20:06.547
A pragmatic standard is contract-first either way (proto or OpenAPI), gRPC for hot paths and streaming, and REST elsewhere, with shared observability and deadline propagation.

1412
01:20:07.897 --> 01:20:09.006
Question 20.

1413
01:20:09.286 --> 01:20:14.683
How do you design APIs for long-running operations (bulk price upload, refunds)?

1414
01:20:14.963 --> 01:20:16.329
Thirty-second answer:

1415
01:20:16.609 --> 01:20:29.558
Accept with 202 Accepted and a Location to an operation resource (/operations/id) that exposes status, progress, result or errors, with idempotency on the submit.

1416
01:20:29.838 --> 01:20:33.145
Offer webhook or SSE notification on completion.

1417
01:20:33.425 --> 01:20:38.225
Make operations cancellable where possible, and keep results for a retention window.

1418
01:20:39.575 --> 01:20:40.471
Part 9.

1419
01:20:40.751 --> 01:20:42.159
Summary and Practice.

1420
01:20:43.409 --> 01:20:44.497
Key takeaways.

1421
01:20:44.777 --> 01:20:49.278
An API is a long-lived contract over an unreliable network.

1422
01:20:49.558 --> 01:20:53.227
Design it to be retry-safe, evolvable and hard to misuse.

1423
01:20:53.507 --> 01:20:59.054
Model resources with correct methods, and use explicit actions for business state transitions.

1424
01:20:59.334 --> 01:21:07.121
Use status codes the client can act on, and RFC 9457 Problem Details with stable codes.

1425
01:21:07.401 --> 01:21:11.539
Keyset pagination with an opaque, signed cursor and a matching index:

1426
01:21:11.819 --> 01:21:13.291
O(limit) and stable.

1427
01:21:14.641 --> 01:21:29.084
Every mutation takes an Idempotency-Key: claim with a unique insert, fingerprint the request, complete in the same transaction, replay identically, return 409 while in progress and 422 on reuse.

1428
01:21:29.364 --> 01:21:33.929
ETags do caching (304) and concurrency (412).

1429
01:21:34.209 --> 01:21:36.983
Implement writes as atomic conditional updates.

1430
01:21:37.263 --> 01:21:38.564
Evolve additively.

1431
01:21:38.844 --> 01:21:45.927
Use a URI major version for public APIs, tolerant readers, and CI-enforced breaking-change checks.

1432
01:21:46.207 --> 01:21:56.404
REST for public and partner APIs, GraphQL for flexible client composition (with batching and cost limits), gRPC for internal hot paths.

1433
01:21:57.754 --> 01:22:03.578
Gateway for edge concerns, BFF per client for composition, no domain rules in either.

1434
01:22:03.858 --> 01:22:10.365
Webhooks are signed, at-least-once, deduplicated by event id, retried with backoff, and replayable.

1435
01:22:10.645 --> 01:22:13.866
Receivers verify the raw body and acknowledge fast.

1436
01:22:15.216 --> 01:22:16.539
Decision cheat sheet.

1437
01:22:16.819 --> 01:22:18.163
Here is a quick table:

1438
01:22:18.443 --> 01:22:19.552
Need, and Choose.

1439
01:22:19.832 --> 01:22:21.667
Retry-safe create or action:

1440
01:22:21.947 --> 01:22:25.851
Idempotency-Key + DB claim + same-tx completion.

1441
01:22:26.131 --> 01:22:27.560
Prevent lost updates:

1442
01:22:27.840 --> 01:22:33.152
Strong ETag + If-Match (428 if missing) + conditional UPDATE.

1443
01:22:33.432 --> 01:22:35.245
History, feeds, exports:

1444
01:22:35.525 --> 01:22:37.872
Keyset pagination with signed cursor.

1445
01:22:38.152 --> 01:22:39.944
Admin table with page numbers:

1446
01:22:40.224 --> 01:22:41.931
Offset (small data only).

1447
01:22:42.211 --> 01:22:43.981
Public or partner API:

1448
01:22:44.261 --> 01:22:48.229
REST + OpenAPI-first + URI major version.

1449
01:22:48.509 --> 01:22:50.707
Mobile screens across many services:

1450
01:22:50.987 --> 01:22:53.867
BFF, or GraphQL with persisted queries.

1451
01:22:54.147 --> 01:22:58.349
Internal high-QPS or streaming: gRPC with deadlines.

1452
01:22:58.629 --> 01:22:59.803
Long-running work:

1453
01:23:00.083 --> 01:23:04.904
202 + operation resource + webhook or SSE completion.

1454
01:23:06.254 --> 01:23:08.025
Notify external systems:

1455
01:23:08.305 --> 01:23:10.673
Signed, retried, replayable webhooks.

1456
01:23:12.023 --> 01:23:13.153
Exercises.

1457
01:23:13.433 --> 01:23:14.777
Here is a quick table:

1458
01:23:15.057 --> 01:23:16.913
Band, and Exercise, and Hint.

1459
01:23:17.193 --> 01:23:18.025
B1.

1460
01:23:18.305 --> 01:23:19.372
Exercise:

1461
01:23:19.652 --> 01:23:23.855
Run L1, then add a list with status filter to the cursor logic.

1462
01:23:24.135 --> 01:23:24.817
Hint:

1463
01:23:25.097 --> 01:23:27.444
The filter applies before limit + 1.

1464
01:23:27.724 --> 01:23:28.556
B1.

1465
01:23:28.836 --> 01:23:29.903
Exercise:

1466
01:23:30.183 --> 01:23:34.279
Write the Problem Details JSON for slot no longer available.

1467
01:23:34.559 --> 01:23:35.241
Hint:

1468
01:23:35.521 --> 01:23:37.249
Pick type, code, retryable.

1469
01:23:37.529 --> 01:23:38.404
B2.

1470
01:23:38.684 --> 01:23:39.751
Exercise:

1471
01:23:40.031 --> 01:23:45.492
Add If-None-Match, then 304 support to L3's GET or orders/id.

1472
01:23:45.772 --> 01:23:46.455
Hint:

1473
01:23:46.735 --> 01:23:48.975
WebRequest.checkNotModified.

1474
01:23:49.255 --> 01:23:50.129
B2.

1475
01:23:50.409 --> 01:23:51.476
Exercise:

1476
01:23:51.756 --> 01:23:59.457
Write an OpenAPI 3.1 spec for POST or orders/id/cancel with all its responses.

1477
01:23:59.737 --> 01:24:00.420
Hint:

1478
01:24:00.700 --> 01:24:06.161
200, 404, 409, 412, 428.

1479
01:24:06.441 --> 01:24:07.316
B3.

1480
01:24:07.596 --> 01:24:08.663
Exercise:

1481
01:24:08.943 --> 01:24:15.983
Add crash-takeover to the L3 IT: kill the owner mid-request (simulate by inserting a stale INPROGRESS row).

1482
01:24:16.263 --> 01:24:18.055
Hint: lockeduntil in the past.

1483
01:24:19.405 --> 01:24:20.279
B3.

1484
01:24:20.559 --> 01:24:21.626
Exercise:

1485
01:24:21.906 --> 01:24:24.445
Implement previous-page cursors for L3.

1486
01:24:24.725 --> 01:24:25.407
Hint:

1487
01:24:25.687 --> 01:24:29.122
